<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-interior-closure-and-boundary-of-complements" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Interior, closure, and boundary of complements
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> U \subseteq X </m> be a subset of a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \Int \of{ X \setminus U } = X \setminus \Cl \of{ U } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Consider a point <m> x \in X </m>, and suppose that <m> x \in \Int \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is an interior point of the complement <m> X \setminus U </m>. Then <m> N = X \setminus U </m> is a neighborhood of <m> x </m>, and of course
        
        <md>
          N \cap U = \of{ X \setminus U } \cap U = \emptyset
        </md>.
        
        Thus <m> x \in X \setminus \Cl \of{ U } </m> is not a point of closure of <m> U </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> x \in X \setminus \Cl \of{ U } </m> is not a point of closure of <m> U </m>. Then <m> N \cap U = \emptyset </m> for some neighborhood <m> N </m> of <m> x </m> in <m> X </m>. In particular, <m> N \subseteq X \setminus U </m>. Since <m> N </m> is a neighborhood of <m> x </m> in <m> X </m>, so is <m> X \setminus U </m>, and so <m> x \in \Int \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is an interior point of <m> X \setminus U </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in \Int \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is an interior point of the complement <m> X \setminus U </m> if and only if <m> x \in X \setminus \Cl \of{ U } </m> is not a point of closure of <m> U </m>. Thus <m> \Int \of{ X \setminus U } = X \setminus \Cl \of{ U } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } = X \setminus \Int \of{ U } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Applying the argument in (a) to the complement <m> X \setminus U </m>, we observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } \amp = X \setminus \of{ X \setminus \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = X \setminus \Int \of{ X \setminus \of{ X \setminus U } } = X \setminus \Int \of{ U }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \Bd \of{ X \setminus U } = \Bd \of{ U } = \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Consider a point <m> x \in X </m>, and suppose first that <m> x \in \Bd \of{ U } </m> is a boundary point of <m> U </m>. Then <m> N \cap U \neq \emptyset </m> for any neighborhood <m> N </m> of <m> x </m> in <m> X </m>, and so <m> x \in \Cl \of{ U } </m> is a point of closure of <m> U </m> in <m> x </m>. On the other hand,
        
        <md>
          N \cap \of{ X \setminus U } = N \setminus U \neq \emptyset
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> x \in \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is a point of closure of the complement <m> X \setminus U </m>. Thus <m> x \in \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> x \in \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m>, and let <m> N </m> be a neighborhood of <m> x </m> in <m> X </m>. Since <m> x \in \Cl \of{ U } </m> is a point of closure of <m> U </m>, <m> N \cap U \neq \emptyset </m>. Moreover, since <m> x \in \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is a point of closure of the complement <m> X \setminus U </m>,
        
        <md>
          N \setminus U = N \cap \of{ X \setminus U } \neq \emptyset
        </md>.
        
        Thus <m> x \in \Bd \of{ U } </m> is a boundary point of <m> U </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> x \in \Bd \of{ U } </m> is a boundary point of <m> U </m> if and only if <m> x \in \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m> is a point of closure of both <m> U </m> and its complement <m> X \setminus U </m>. Thus <m> \Bd \of{ U } = \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } </m>. We observe that this implies that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \Bd \of{ U } \amp = \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } 
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \Cl \of{ X \setminus U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus \of{ X \setminus U } } = \Bd \of{ X \setminus U }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m> \Bd \of{ U } = \Cl \of{ U } \setminus \Int \of{ U } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Applying the arguments in (b) and (c), we observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \Bd \of{ U } \amp = \Cl \of{ U } \cap \Cl \of{ X \setminus U }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \Cl \of{ U } \cap \of{ X \setminus \Int \of{ U } } = \Cl \of{ U } \setminus \Int \of{ U }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
